   "project": "fieldnotes",
   "reply": "`delete INDEX` now works, and `python tools/check.py` passes: all 6 tests and the line-length rule, exit 0. I ran it with `NOTES_HOME` set to a temp directory.\n\n**Red:** before implementing, the check failed with 2 failures and 2 errors. The two `store.delete` tests errored with `AttributeError: module 'fieldnotes.store' has no attribute 'delete'`, and the two CLI tests got exit code 2 instead of 0 and 1. The third CLI test, non-integer input, already passed because unknown commands return 2.\n\n**Green:**\n- **`fieldnotes/store.py`:** a new `delete(index)` removes and returns the note at that position. It raises `IndexError` if the index is out of range, including negatives, and leaves the file unchanged in that case.\n- **`fieldnotes/cli.py`:**\n  - `delete INDEX` prints `deleted: <text>` and exits 0.\n  - An out-of-range index prints `no note at index N` to stderr and exits 1.\n  - A non-integer index prints the usage message and exits 2.\n  - I pulled the usage text into a `USAGE` constant that now lists `delete INDEX`, and updated the module docstring.\n- **Tests:** two new cases in `tests/test_store.py` and a new `tests/test_cli.py` with three.\n\n**Decision for you:** INDEX is 0-based, because `list` doesn't print numbers and I didn't want to change its output. If you'd rather have 1-based indices, I can make `list` show numbers and shift `delete` to match.",
